from lessons2 import L, les
les(id="usb1",t="USB fundamentals: speeds, packets, endpoints, transfers, descriptors",tag="USB",
an="USB is a strict classroom. The host is the teacher: nobody speaks unless asked. Each device has numbered mailboxes (endpoints). Control mail is for paperwork, bulk for big parcels that can wait, interrupt for small urgent notes checked on a schedule, isochronous for a live radio broadcast where late is as bad as lost.",
j=[("Host / device","The host (PC, hub controller) starts every transaction; devices only answer."),("Endpoint","A numbered data pipe on a device, IN (to host) or OUT. EP0 is the control endpoint."),("Descriptor","A small structure describing the device, configs, interfaces, endpoints."),("Interface / alt setting","A function (e.g. video streaming); alt settings let it switch bandwidth (alt0 = zero bandwidth)."),("Microframe","125 µs time slot in high-speed USB (8 per 1 ms frame).")],
d="""<h4>Speeds</h4><p>Low 1.5 Mb/s, Full 12 Mb/s, High 480 Mb/s (USB 2.0), SuperSpeed 5 Gb/s, SuperSpeed+ 10/20 Gb/s (USB 3.x). USB 3 adds separate SuperSpeed wires and companion descriptors (<code>bMaxBurst</code>, <code>Mult</code>).</p>
<h4>Transfer types</h4><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Guarantee</th><th>Conference device use</th></tr><tr><td>Control</td><td>reliable, EP0</td><td>enumeration, UVC controls (brightness, PTZ), UAC volume</td></tr><tr><td>Bulk</td><td>reliable, leftover bandwidth</td><td>CDC serial, mass storage, ADB, MTP, sometimes UVC bulk mode</td></tr><tr><td>Interrupt</td><td>reliable, bounded latency</td><td>HID buttons, UVC status</td></tr><tr><td>Isochronous</td><td>reserved bandwidth, no retries</td><td>UVC video, UAC audio</td></tr></table>
<h4>Bandwidth math (high-speed isoc)</h4><p><code>wMaxPacketSize</code> bits 0-10 = bytes per transaction (≤1024), bits 11-12 = extra transactions per microframe. 1024 × 3 × 8000/s = 24.576 MB/s ≈ 196 Mb/s max for one HS isoc endpoint. A 1080p30 YUY2 stream needs 1920×1080×2×30 = 124 MB/s → impossible on USB 2 isoc, so conference cameras send MJPEG/H.264 over USB 2 or use USB 3.</p>
<h4>Descriptor hierarchy</h4><p>Device → Configuration (wTotalLength covers everything below) → [IAD groups interfaces of one function] → Interface (alt settings) → class-specific descriptors → Endpoints. Lab 04 parses this exactly; in our QEMU run it parsed the real descriptors of the gadget from Lab 06.</p>""",
anim=("seq","One high-speed microframe (125 µs)",["Host","Isoc EP (video)","Interrupt EP (HID)","Bulk EP (serial)"],[(0,1,"IN token → 1024 B"),(0,1,"IN token → 1024 B"),(0,1,"IN token → 1024 B (mult=3)"),(0,2,"IN token (interval due) → NAK/data"),(0,3,"IN → bulk fills leftover time"),(0,0,"next microframe")]),
q=[("Why does a USB 2 webcam send MJPEG for 1080p30?","Uncompressed YUY2 1080p30 needs ~124 MB/s; one HS isoc endpoint maxes around 24.6 MB/s."),("What does alt setting 0 with zero endpoints mean for UVC streaming?","Zero bandwidth: the interface reserves nothing until the host selects a streaming alt setting.")],lab="04-usb-descriptor-parser")

les(id="usbenum",t="Enumeration and chapter 9 requests",tag="USB",
anim=("seq","USB enumeration, step by step",["Host (xHCI + hub)","Device (UDC + gadget)"],[(1,0,"attach: D+ pull-up"),(0,1,"bus reset; HS chirp"),(0,1,"GET_DESCRIPTOR(DEVICE, 64) @ addr 0"),(1,0,"18-byte device desc (bMaxPacketSize0)"),(0,1,"SET_ADDRESS(7)"),(1,0,"status ACK → now addr 7"),(0,1,"GET_DESCRIPTOR(CONFIG, 9)"),(1,0,"wTotalLength = 107"),(0,1,"GET_DESCRIPTOR(CONFIG, 107)"),(0,1,"GET_DESCRIPTOR(STRING ...)"),(0,1,"SET_CONFIGURATION(1)"),(1,0,"endpoints live; class drivers bind")]),
an="Enumeration is a new employee's first morning: show up at the front desk (attach), get a temporary visitor badge (address 0), tell HR your basics (device descriptor), receive an employee number (SET_ADDRESS), hand in the full résumé (configuration), and get assigned to a team (SET_CONFIGURATION).",
j=[("SETUP packet","8-byte control request: bmRequestType, bRequest, wValue, wIndex, wLength."),("Chapter 9","The USB spec chapter defining standard requests and device states."),("STALL","A device's 'I can't do that' response on an endpoint."),("UDC","USB Device Controller: the hardware + driver that lets a board act as a USB device.")],
d="""<h4>Device states</h4><p>Attached → Powered → Default (addr 0 after reset) → Address → Configured (→ Suspended when bus idles &gt;3 ms). Lab 05 simulates this and the retry path when SET_ADDRESS fails.</p>
<h4>Reading host logs</h4><pre>usb 1-1: new high-speed USB device number 2 using xhci_hcd
usb 1-1: device descriptor read/64, error -71     ← -EPROTO: signal integrity / timing
usb 1-1: device not accepting address 2, error -71
usb 1-1: unable to enumerate USB device</pre>
<p><b>-71 (EPROTO)</b> usually means electrical problems (cable, VBUS, PHY tuning, missing ESD/termination), or device firmware too slow to respond. <b>-110 (ETIMEDOUT)</b>: device stopped answering. <b>-32 (EPIPE)</b>: STALL, often an unsupported request.</p>
<h4>What the device side must get right</h4><ul><li>Answer EP0 within spec timing (status stage ≤ 50 ms for most requests, SET_ADDRESS settle 2 ms).</li><li>Return exactly wLength or less; a short packet ends the data stage.</li><li>wTotalLength must equal the real size; Windows is less forgiving than Linux.</li><li>Microsoft OS descriptors / BOS descriptor for USB 2.1+ and WinUSB auto-install.</li><li>Re-enumeration when switching functions (Android mode switch: unbind UDC, change functions, rebind).</li></ul>
<p><b>Windows check:</b> USBView and the Windows USB ETW trace; <b>macOS:</b> <code>ioreg -p IOUSB -l</code>. Conference devices must work on all three hosts: keep a host-matrix test.</p>""",
q=[("dmesg shows 'device descriptor read/64, error -71'. First suspects?","Electrical/PHY: cable, connector, VBUS, PHY tuning parameters, or the UDC not running yet when D+ is pulled up."),("Why does the host read only 9 bytes of the config descriptor first?","To learn wTotalLength, then request the full set in one read.")],lab="05-usb-enum-sim")

les(id="usbclass",t="USB classes for conferencing: UVC, UAC, HID, CDC",tag="USB",
an="Classes are standard job descriptions. If your device says 'I'm a UVC camera', every OS already has an employee handbook (built-in driver) for that role, so no custom driver install. That is why conference bars 'just work' when plugged into any laptop.",
j=[("UVC","USB Video Class: cameras. Spec 1.1/1.5; formats YUY2, NV12, MJPEG, H.264 (frame-based)."),("UAC","USB Audio Class 1/2/3: mics and speakers."),("HID","Human Interface Device: buttons, telephony controls (hook switch, mute) via report descriptors."),("CDC ACM / NCM","Communications class: serial port / network over USB."),("Probe/Commit","UVC negotiation where host and device agree on format, frame size, interval and max payload.")],
d="""<h4>UVC in depth</h4><ul><li><b>VideoControl interface</b>: units/terminals topology (Camera Terminal for PTZ/exposure, Processing Unit for brightness, Extension Units for vendor features like auto-framing).</li><li><b>VideoStreaming interface</b>: formats and frames; alt settings with different isoc packet sizes.</li><li><b>Probe/Commit</b>: host sends PROBE SET_CUR, device answers GET_CUR with <code>dwMaxPayloadTransferSize</code>, host COMMITs, then selects an alt setting that fits. Bugs here = 'camera opens but black'.</li><li><b>Payload header</b>: every packet begins with a 2-12 byte header: FID toggles per frame, EOF bit ends a frame, optional PTS/SCR timestamps (needed for A/V sync).</li></ul>
<h4>UAC</h4><p>UAC1 (full-speed era, works everywhere), UAC2 (high-speed, higher rates, clock entities), UAC3 (power saving, BADD). Choose sync type: async with feedback EP is best for a device with its own crystal. Volume/mute controls via Feature Unit.</p>
<h4>HID for conferencing</h4><p>Teams/Zoom certification needs Telephony page (0x0B) controls: hook switch, phone mute, LEDs (host → device output reports) so the device mute light follows the app. Report descriptor mistakes = buttons ignored on one OS.</p>
<h4>Composite devices</h4><p>Combine functions with IADs (device class 0xEF/0x02/0x01). Order interfaces carefully; changing interface numbers between firmware versions breaks Windows' cached drivers (it caches by VID/PID/bcdDevice/interface number). Bump bcdDevice when descriptors change.</p>""",
anim=("seq","UVC probe/commit then streaming",["Host uvcvideo","Device UVC function","Userspace UVC app"],[(0,1,"SET_CUR PROBE (format, frame, interval)"),(1,2,"event: setup request"),(2,1,"fill probe struct (maxPayload)"),(0,1,"GET_CUR PROBE"),(0,1,"SET_CUR COMMIT"),(0,1,"SET_INTERFACE alt=1 (bandwidth)"),(1,2,"STREAMON event"),(2,1,"queue frames (V4L2 output)"),(1,0,"isoc payloads: header FID/EOF + data")]),
q=[("Camera enumerates but app shows black. What part of UVC to inspect first?","Probe/commit values (maxPayload vs chosen alt), whether the gadget app queued frames after STREAMON, payload header FID/EOF bits."),("After a firmware update, Windows shows the camera with a yellow bang. Likely cause?","Descriptors changed without bumping bcdDevice/PID; Windows applied a cached driver config to the new layout.")],lab=None)

les(id="gadget",t="Linux USB gadget stack: UDC, composite, configfs, FunctionFS, Android USB HAL",tag="USB",
an="configfs is a LEGO baseplate. Each USB function (camera, mic, serial, buttons) is a brick. You stack the bricks into a configuration and then 'plug the baseplate into the wall' by writing the controller name into the UDC file. Unplug = write empty string.",
j=[("Gadget","Linux name for device-mode USB."),("libcomposite","Kernel library that combines several functions into one device."),("configfs","A filesystem (/sys/kernel/config) where creating directories creates kernel objects."),("FunctionFS","Lets a userspace program implement a USB function (Android uses it for ADB/MTP)."),("DWC3","Synopsys DesignWare USB3 controller, used in Qualcomm, NXP i.MX8 and many SoCs."),("dummy_hcd","A virtual host+device controller pair in software, for testing gadgets on one PC.")],
d="""<h4>Stack layers</h4><p>UDC driver (dwc3, chipidea, cdns3) → <code>udc-core</code> → composite (<code>libcomposite</code>) → function drivers (<code>usb_f_uvc, usb_f_uac2, usb_f_hid, usb_f_acm, usb_f_ncm, usb_f_fs</code>) → userspace (UVC streaming app, adbd, mtp) → Android USB HAL (<code>android.hardware.usb.gadget</code>) and init scripts that write configfs.</p>
<h4>Lab 06, verified</h4><p>On a stock Debian 6.12 kernel in QEMU, the script created a composite gadget and bound it to dummy_hcd; the host side enumerated it: <code>cdc_acm 1-1:1.0: ttyACM0</code>, <code>hid-generic ... USB HID v1.01 Device [Lab Corp Lab Conference Cam]</code>. UVC + UAC2 failed to bind with -EINVAL because <b>dummy_hcd has no isochronous support</b>: a real lesson. Test isoc functions on a real board (DWC3) or with a USB analyzer.</p>
<h4>UVC gadget specifics</h4><p><code>usb_f_uvc</code> creates a V4L2 <i>output</i> device on the gadget side; a userspace app (e.g. the open-source uvc-gadget / libcamera uvc-gadget, or your product's daemon) handles UVC events (probe/commit, STREAMON) and queues frames from the ISP/encoder. Tune <code>streaming_maxpacket</code>, <code>streaming_maxburst</code> (USB 3), <code>streaming_interval</code>.</p>
<h4>Android</h4><ul><li>The framework sets <code>sys.usb.config</code>/calls the USB gadget HAL (<code>setCurrentUsbFunctions</code>); vendor init .rc files or the HAL write configfs.</li><li>ADB and MTP are FunctionFS functions served by adbd/MtpServer.</li><li>Typec HAL (<code>android.hardware.usb</code>) reports port roles and handles role swap.</li></ul>
<h4>DWC3 debugging</h4><p><code>/sys/kernel/debug/usb/&lt;dwc3&gt;/</code> has regdump, link_state, ep queues; enable tracepoints: <code>echo 1 &gt; /sys/kernel/tracing/events/dwc3/enable</code> then read <code>trace</code> to see every ep0 request and TRB completion.</p>""",
anim=("flow","configfs gadget build (Lab 06)",["mkdir usb_gadget/polydemo","write idVendor/idProduct/strings","mkdir configs/c.1","mkdir functions/uvc.0 uac2.0 acm.0 hid.0","ln -s functions → configs/c.1","echo dummy_udc.0 > UDC","host enumerates"],["gadget object created","device descriptor values","configuration + MaxPower","function instances","compose the configuration","bind = D+ pull-up","ttyACM0 + hidraw0 appear"]),
q=[("Gadget bind fails with -22 on dummy_hcd only when UVC is included. Why?","dummy_hcd doesn't support isochronous endpoints; UVC/UAC2 need isoc. Use a real UDC."),("How does Android expose ADB over the same port as your camera function?","adbd implements ADB in userspace through FunctionFS (ffs.adb), linked into the same composite configuration.")],lab="06-configfs-uvc-uac-gadget")
